  "response_text": "The stack is treated as a continuous array, and thus you must start counting from the MSP initial value. From the MSP initial value, we can move downwards in our stack to the first thread by subtracting the main stack size (0x400). This is because stack memory on the Cortex-M4 grows downwards. Then to get to the second thread PSP, we subtract the thread stack size. From this point, we continue to subtract the thread stack size to get to the next thread PSP. It is important to note that the stack (MSP and PSP) must be aligned to 8 bytes, thus the starting address must be in multiples of 8. Here, a stack size of 0x400 meets that requirement. If the stack size is not a multiple of the alignment, it could result in a hard fault.\\nThis can be summarized with the equation:\\nADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL - MAIN_STACK_SIZE - (j-1)THREAD_STACK_SIZE\\nWhere j is the stack that you want to address. Using j = 1 would give ADDR_Stack_1 = MSP_INIT_VAL - MAIN_STACK_SIZE, which means adding the main stack size to the initial MSP value. This makes sense. Using j = 0 is an edge case that could result in errors if MAIN_STACK_SIZE does not equal THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\\n",
